[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports said otherwise.
Read one by one, they describe more than one bug. Each problem below has its own ticket, with its own
count.

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. `parser` is fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`).
`hint` won't be fixed: the booking-v3 form is retired. Nothing in that update touches `shift`.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but <code>shift</code> is not fixed. A date that moves by one day goes to its own
ticket, not to #4471.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{
  parser: { href: 'https://github.com/nexty5870/ticket-mega/issues/1', state: 'fixed' },
  hint: { href: 'https://github.com/nexty5870/ticket-mega/issues/2', state: "won't fix" },
  shift: { href: 'https://github.com/nexty5870/ticket-mega/issues/3', state: 'open' },
}} />

The counts follow the desk's own view, as exported. The export's pages overlap, so its rows
(<ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="rows" />) hold <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports. Each report
is filed under the cause its tenant and the way it came in point to (`causes.ts`), not under its
intake tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the table shows how often that guess is
wrong. `shift` reports stay filed under `parser` as well, so the rows add up to more than the total.

## `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="parser" /> reports. Every tenant on file writes the day first. The
shared parser read every date month first, so a booking for 7 April went out on 4 July. This is the
bug #4471 was opened for, and it is fixed. Reports from booking-v3 tenants count here when they came
in by app or by email.

## `hint`: booking-v3's form tells day-first people to type the month first

It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="hint" /> reports, all through the booking form of tenants that run
`booking-v3`. That form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says, and the
form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The date is wrong before the parser ever sees it, so
the parser fix does not touch this one. The form is retired, so the ticket closes as won't fix.

## `shift`: the date moves by one day

It covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="shift" /> reports. Here the month does not flip; the day moves by one:

- "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May."
- "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."
- "The invite arrived before I made the booking."

That reads as a time zone bug: a date stored as a moment in one zone and shown in another. The desk
noted these as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`) and files them under `parser` by tenant. The parser
fix does not move them back, and closing #4471 did not close them.

## Keeping this page right

The numbers on this page are computed when it renders, from `reports-4471.json` and
`desk-notes.json`. When the desk merges more reports, or notes another one-day move, the table and
the counts follow. A report from a tenant that is not on file shows up as `unplaced`.

## Lessons

- Same subject is not the same bug. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on
  subject alone and expects engineering to split later. Here nobody split, and the ticket was marked
  known.
- Before you mark a big ticket known, read its reports, not only its subject lines.
- A closed ticket is not a fixed problem. Check each problem against what the fix touched.

